    "last_assistant_text": "The proto fuzzer takes a protobuf `Function` message, converts it to Ruby source code via `protoConverter::FunctionToString`, then runs it through `mrb_load_string`. The protobuf input generates a function with the structure:\n```ruby\ndef foo()\nvar_0 = 1\n... statements ...\nend\nfoo\n```\n\nThis is very constrained — it generates a single function `foo()` with assignments, if/else, ternary expressions, and builtin calls. It can't generate recursive functions, custom classes, or `Exception.exception` calls.\n\nThe proto format doesn't support the patterns I need (recursion, `RuntimeError.exception`). But the fuzzer takes raw protobuf binary, which then gets converted to Ruby. Let me think about what Ruby code the proto can generate that might trigger this vulnerability differently.\n\nActually, wait. Looking more carefully at the runtime output, it seems like the fuzzer is trying to parse the input as a protobuf text format first, then as binary. The key line is:\n\n```\n[libprotobuf WARNING ...] Warning parsing text-format ruby_fuzzer.Function: 1:5: Message type \"ruby_fuzzer.Function\" has no field named \"def\".\n```\n\nThis suggests the input is parsed as text-format protobuf. But the `DEFINE_PROTO_FUZZER` macro typically accepts binary protobuf. Let me check what `DEFINE_PROTO_FUZZER` actually does with the input...\n\nThe `DEFINE_PROTO_FUZZER` macro from libprotobuf-mutator accepts **protobuf binary format** as input. When running with a corpus file, it deserializes the binary protobuf into a `Function` message. \n\nBut the proto format is very limited. It doesn't support function definitions beyond a single `foo()`, doesn't support recursion, and doesn't support calling `RuntimeError.exception`. The generated Ruby code is always:\n```ruby\ndef foo()\nvar_0 = 1\n...simple statements...\nend\nfoo\n```\n\nThere's no way to generate deep recursion or exception creation through this proto grammar. But the ossfuzz issue was found by this fuzzer, so maybe the bug can be triggered through a different code path.\n\nLet me reconsider the vulnerability. The description says \"mrb_funcall_with_block / related stack copy logic\". Let me look for other callers of `mrb_funcall_with_block` or `mrb_funcall_argv` that might be triggered through simpler code patterns.",
